okay let's do some audience questions  for the week some good ones I'm looking  forward to answering today first one is  from Sam hey David why does your show  have a mostly male audience well this is  actually it's not just our show this is  a general dynamic for shows like ours in  general and this is something I've  talked to a lot of people about I know a  Sam cedar and I have talked about this  and I've talked about it with a lot of  folks over at the young turks and it is  sort of a tough nut to crack and what it  really comes down to is that  fundamentally online news and politics  has a mostly male audience so it's not  so much about the show's it's about the  format I don't have a single close  female friend I can think of that  watches my show or shows like mine I  mean period they you know they're sort  of tangentially aware of my show because  they see stuff on Facebook for example  but nobody that I know who is is a woman  regularly says oh yeah I watched The  Young Turks or Sam cedar or whatever and  it is really common for shows like the  david pakman show to have anywhere from  60 to 80 percent male audiences I think  our podcast audience is has a little  more female to male ratio in it the  YouTube channel is more male than the  podcast for example hard to get data  from radio or from television and part  of it is the format which is it's just a  format that is more heavily watched by  men part of it is also story selection  because if you look at studies about  which stories were more followed by men  versus more followed by women when you  look at from Pew Research for example  the news stories that were more followed  by women than men it's all stories that  we didn't cover okay so the coal miners  in Utah was a news story that was  followed more by women than by men  contaminated pet food we didn't cover it  maybe it got a mention on the bonus show  the Chinese toy recall a variety of  kidnappings of kids that I don't even  remember at this point over the years  the story about drug-resistant staph  infections spreading we didn't cover or  barely covered any of those stories so  it's also story selection if you look at  stories like for example at Venezuela  the Supreme Court immigration Iran those  are stories that were more heavily  followed by men than by women so in  short you've got a format that in  general is more watched by Mendon by  women and then you have story selection  within our show which also seems to not  be including some of those stories that  were followed more close closely by  women and that's sort of where you end  up now I am NOT considering it sort of a  foregone conclusion or sort of fait  accompli we are actively talking about  how can we increase our female following  and you know there's little things that  I do  one is with voicemails when we get  voicemails from women I make sure to try  to get more of those voices represented  because it creates this vicious circle  if everybody you hear leaving a  voicemail is male and you're female that  might be a disincentive to even call in  so same thing with phone calls we've got  some great female callers including  valve from Agawam who we love and when  she calls then we just try to you know  include more female voices and also with  guest selection there is actually a big  discussion that happens in general when  doing analyses of news media that  they're not only is there a big  corporate skew of so-called experts on  news media there's also a major male  skew and so since overwhelmingly the  guests that we get pitched are men we  also make sure that for the guests that  we decide to go after and invite to the  show that we're including a mix and  we've had some really great female  guests as well so that's what we're  doing but there are some sort of  systemic issues that explain a lot of  why that is now if you say why is it  that online news and politics does not  appeal to women as much as it does to  men I don't have the answer and I'd be  curious to hear from you what you think  might explain it
 

